verses.text:
Then he took some of the seed of the land
and planted it in fertile soil;
he set it like a willow beside abundant waters.{a}

She brought up one of her cubs;
he became a young lion.
He learned to tear prey;
he devoured people.

Nations got word of him;
in their pit he was taken.
With hooks they hauled him off
to Egypt's land.{a}

He prowled among the lions;
he became a young lion.
He learned to tear prey;
he devoured people.

But in fury it was torn out
and flung to the earth.
Its fruit was parched by the east wind;
its mighty boughs broke away and dried;
fire devoured them.

sharpened for slaughter,
polished to flash like lightning!
Shall we rejoice? The scepter of my son despises every tree.{b}

so that hearts may melt
and the stumbling may be many.
At all their gates I have set the slaughter of the sword.{e}
Ah! It is made to flash like lightning;
it is grasped for slaughter.

With drunkenness and grief you will be filled;
a cup of ruin and desolation
is your sister Samaria's cup.

Tell a parable to the rebellious house and say to them, 'This is what the Lord Yahweh says:
Set the pot on the fire, set it on;
pour water into it as well.

Gather its pieces into it,
every choice piece, the thigh and the shoulder;
pack it full of the best bones.

Take the choicest of the flock;
pile the bones{b} under it.
Bring it to a rolling boil;
let its bones seethe in it.

"'So the Lord Yahweh says this:
Woe to the city of blood,
the pot with corrosion{c} inside,
whose corrosion will not come out!
Take it out, piece after piece;
cast no lot for it.{d}

It has exhausted my efforts;{f}
its heavy corrosion will not leave it.
Let its corrosion go into the fire!

From oaks of Bashan
they made your oars;
your deck they made of ivory
inlaid in cypress{b} from the coasts of Kittim.{c}

Your sail was of Egypt's embroidered linen,
spread out to be your ensign;
your canopy was violet and purple
from the shores of Elishah.{d}

They will shave their heads for you
and put on sackcloth;
they will weep over you in bitterness of soul,
with bitter mourning.{v}

All the inhabitants of the coastlands
are appalled at you;
their kings shudder with horror;
their faces are troubled.

The merchants among the peoples hiss at you;
you have become a terror,
and you will be no more forever.'"

"'The Lord Yahweh says this:
I will wipe out the idols
and make an end of Memphis's worthless gods.{d}
No prince will rise again
from Egypt's land,
and I will put fear in Egypt's land.

I will set Egypt ablaze;
Pelusium will writhe in pain,
Thebes will be split open,
and Memphis will meet foes in broad day.{h}

On's{i} youths and those of Pi-beseth{j}
will be cut down by the sword,
and the towns themselves{k} will go into captivity.
